Best Use Cases: Pie charts are most effective for displaying a small number of categories and providing a general sense of relative contributions, such as market share or budget distribution. 

Limitations: They are generally discouraged for precise comparisons, datasets with many categories, or continuous data, as human vision is better at comparing lengths (bar charts) than angles and areas. 

Variants: Common variations include doughnut charts (with a central hole for additional info), exploded pie charts (to highlight specific slices), and 3D pie charts (often criticized for distorting perception)